<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="European Commission">
  <data key="d0">European Commission</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The European Commission is a crucial governing body and the executive branch of the European Union (EU), tasked with a range of significant responsibilities that include proposing legislation, implementing decisions, and overseeing the daily operations of the EU. This entity plays a vital role in upholding treaties and managing various policy areas, with a particular focus on ensuring safety and compliance in the fast-evolving field of artificial intelligence (AI). The Commission is actively involved in the development of the AI Act, a regulatory initiative aimed at guaranteeing the safety and reliability of AI applications throughout the EU, reflecting its commitment to balancing innovation with public safety.

In addition to its legislative and regulatory roles, the European Commission engages in grant oversight and financial management. It acts as the granting authority for various projects, such as CoEvolution, administering grants, conducting audits, and performing impact evaluations. This role underscores the Commission's essential function in ensuring effective distribution and accountability of EU funding, facilitated through the EU Funding &amp; Tenders Portal.

The European Commission integrates a variety of functions, encompassing grant administration, regulatory compliance, and policy formulation. Its strategic involvement in initiatives like the CoEvolution project further highlights its intent to influence and shape policy strategies within the framework of the European Union. Overall, the European Commission is instrumental in creating and enforcing legislation, managing funding, and advancing the EU's overarching goals.</data>
