  <data key="d0">EU Values</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">EU Values encompass the principles and ethical standards that guide the European Union's policies, particularly regarding technology and AI ethics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-284cd6a9713f3db6c2c93c7cc55157ba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1755420455</data>
</node>
<node id="Adversarial Training">
  <data key="d0">Adversarial Training</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Adversarial Training is a methodology used to enhance AI models' resilience against attacks by exposing them to adversarial examples during training.&lt;SEP&gt;Adversarial Training is a technique used to improve the robustness of machine learning models by training them on adversarial examples to better handle potential attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-20c0cc0a37ebaa1bdc18241b7b7a42f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-284cd6a9713f3db6c2c93c7cc55157ba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1755422749</data>
</node>
<node id="AI Security Assurance Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">AI Security Assurance Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The AI Security Assurance Mechanism records all assessment activities that an AI model undergoes to achieve security and trust, thus generating credentials for the model throughout its lifecycle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65261df93ed337be33236b9008ec65a1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1755420456</data>
</node>
<node id="Organizations across Industries">
  <data key="d0">Organizations across Industries</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Various organizations across industries are identified as beneficiaries of the CoEvolution framework for securing AI models and enhancing cybersecurity management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65261df93ed337be33236b9008ec65a1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1755420456</data>
</node>
<node id="Hardware Vulnerabilities">
  <data key="d0">Hardware Vulnerabilities</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hardware Vulnerabilities refer to potential weaknesses in hardware that could be exploited during AI model design and training, considered in the CoEvolution framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Hardware vulnerabilities refer to potential security weaknesses that can be exploited during the design and training of AI models, emphasizing the need for robust defenses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b977bae012f1a149c35d078f2c64ab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65261df93ed337be33236b9008ec65a1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1755424945</data>
</node>
<node id="Model Extraction">
  <data key="d0">Model Extraction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Model Extraction is a significant threat model involving an attacker attempting to reconstruct or recreate a machine learning model based on its outputs. This process typically entails adversaries employing various techniques to retrieve sensitive information from these models, which ultimately poses threats to both security and privacy. Specifically, model extraction not only focuses on replicating the functional aspects of the machine learning model through its response patterns but also encompasses hardware-based attacks. In these scenarios, an adversary seeks to extract the model's architecture and parameters, thereby creating a broader security risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Model extraction involves an attacker making attempts to recreate a trained machine learning model to gain insights about its behaviors or architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f36d4368a91cfa20fb1fbc19cc60bc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7cc5c6e530f1da15321ba18788990ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65261df93ed337be33236b9008ec65a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c57d3ea1e6c2e634c6f78e88a9a227d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8def759f18995b41ea21003cc5dcc30</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1755425704</data>
</node>
